      <article id="harmful-downloads" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Safety &amp; browsers</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Harmful downloads?</h2>

        <div class="alert alert-info mb-4" role="alert">
          <strong>We do not distribute viruses, trojans, spyware, or malware.</strong>
        </div>

        <div class="index-prose mb-3">
          <p>
            Unfortunately, in their default setup, Chrome and Firefox block many downloads because Google Safe Browsing declares:
            <em>Some pages on this site are unsafe. The site defacto2.net contains harmful content, including pages that: Contain unwanted or malicious software.</em>
            See the <a href="https://transparencyreport.google.com/safe-browsing/search?url=defacto2.net&amp;hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Safe Browsing site status report</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Many Scene intros and cracktros implement decompressing, self-decrypting, and self-modifying techniques, which is also typical behavior for malware.
          </p>
          <p>
            Analyzing suspicious behavior in software is never foolproof, and an over-sensitive
            <a href="https://usa.kaspersky.com/resource-center/definitions/heuristic-analysis" target="_blank" rel="noopener">heuristic scan</a> can report false positive results.
            Some programs and applications offered on Defacto2 get flagged as potentially unwanted software, though we do not believe they are dangerous.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border-top pt-3">
          <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-body-secondary fw-semibold mb-2">To stay safe</h3>
          <ol class="small mb-3 d-flex flex-column gap-2">
            <li>
              <strong>Use the built-in browser emulator when available:</strong> Many MS-DOS intros, cracktros, and magazines on Defacto2 can be run safely inside the browser without executing anything on your computer.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Scan downloads with <a href="https://www.virustotal.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VirusTotal</a>:</strong>
              Keep in mind that packed 1990s executables (UPX, PKLite, Shrinker) often trigger generic heuristic or packer warnings (<code>Generic</code>, <code>PUA</code>, <code>Packer.*</code>) even when harmless. Multiple engines flagging a specific historical virus family (such as <code>CIH</code> or <code>Parite</code>) is a real warning sign.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Never run legacy executables on your primary Windows desktop:</strong> Use an isolated, offline virtual machine or emulator, and keep a clean snapshot to roll back to after testing.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <ul class="small text-body-secondary mb-3 d-flex flex-column gap-1">
            <li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/application-security/application-isolation/windows-sandbox/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Windows Sandbox</a> (Windows 10/11 Pro and Enterprise) for quick, disposable testing of 32-bit Windows executables.</li>
            <li><a href="https://dosbox-staging.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOSBox Staging</a>, <a href="https://dosbox-x.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOSBox-X</a>, or <a href="https://www.dosbox.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOSBox</a> for MS-DOS emulation on all platforms (DOSBox Staging can also run Windows 95 and 98).</li>
            <li><a href="https://www.parallels.com/products/desktop" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Parallels Desktop</a> 18.1 or earlier on Intel Macs, or <a href="https://mac.getutm.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UTM</a> (QEMU) on Apple Silicon Macs.</li>
            <li><a href="https://www.virtualbox.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VirtualBox</a> for Windows Vista and newer (poor support for Windows NT and 2000).</li>
            <li><a href="https://86box.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">86Box</a> or <a href="https://pcem-emulator.co.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PCem</a> for accurate retro PC hardware emulation of DOS and Windows 95/98.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="small text-body-secondary mb-0">
            See <a href="/apps#dos-programs">Running DOS programs</a> and <a href="/apps#old-windows">Running old Windows programs</a> for setup details.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="modification-dates" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Downloads</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Preserve file modification dates</h2>
        <div class="index-prose mb-3">
          <p>
            Unfortunately, web browsers ignore the last modification date metadata when it is attached to a file download.
            However, you can use command-line tools such as <code>wget</code> or <code>curl</code> to preserve the original timestamp.
          </p>
          <p>
            For example, the artifact <q><a href="/f/b32455c">SYMCOLOR.COR</a></q> was last modified in November 1985. To download and preserve its date on your system:
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-dark text-light rounded-2 p-3 overflow-x-auto">
<pre class="font-monospace small mb-0">$ wget --content-disposition https://defacto2.net/d/b32455c
$ curl -LOJR https://defacto2.net/d/b32455c

  Saving 'SYMCOLOR.COR'
  HTTP response 200 OK [https://defacto2.net/d/b32455c]
  SYMCOLOR.COR         100%

$ ls -l SYMCOLOR.COR
  2560 Nov 28  1985  SYMCOLOR.COR</pre>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="shrinker-err" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Windows 9x</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Shrinker.err Dispatcher initialization errors</h2>
        <div class="index-prose mb-3">
          <p>
            This is a common error that occurs with Windows 9x applications compressed using earlier versions of the Shrinker compression library.
            Fortunately, there is an easy solution: decompress the application using either <strong>DeShrink 1.6</strong> or <strong>UnShrinker</strong>.
            DeShrink is a standard Windows application, while UnShrinker uses the command prompt.
          </p>
          <p>
            There will be occasions where these tools cannot decrypt or fix the affected programs.
            In those circumstances, the only solution is to run the affected application on a Windows 95, 98, or ME system.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="border-top pt-3 d-flex flex-wrap align-items-center gap-2">
          <span class="small fw-semibold text-body-secondary me-1">Available tools:</span>
          <a href="/f/a12f23" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary">DeShrink 1.6</a>
          <a href="/f/a2331b" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary">UnShrinker</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="runtime-error-200" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">MS-DOS · Turbo Pascal</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Runtime error 200</h2>
        <div class="index-prose mb-4">
          <p>
            A runtime error 200 message usually occurs with old DOS applications written in Turbo Pascal or Borland Pascal.
            Turbo Pascal had a timing bug that caused its compiled programs to crash when executed on machines running at 200 MHz or faster.
            It is known as the <q>CRT unit issue</q> and can be fixed by patching the affected executable,
            or by running the program in DOSBox with artificially reduced CPU cycles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-body-secondary fw-semibold mb-3">Step-by-step patching guide</h3>
        <ol class="list-group list-group-numbered">
          <li class="list-group-item d-flex justify-content-between align-items-start">
            <div class="ms-2 me-auto">
              <div class="fw-semibold">Download <a href="/f/a43b6b">UNP 4.11</a></div>
              <div class="small text-body-secondary">A DOS executable decompression program.</div>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="list-group-item d-flex justify-content-between align-items-start">
            <div class="ms-2 me-auto">
              <div class="fw-semibold">Download <a href="/f/a33773">CRTFix 1.16</a></div>
              <div class="small text-body-secondary">A CRT timing bug patching program.</div>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="list-group-item d-flex justify-content-between align-items-start">
            <div class="ms-2 me-auto">
              <div class="fw-semibold mb-1">Create a temporary directory</div>
              <code>C:\&gt; MKDIR C:\TPATCH</code>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="list-group-item d-flex justify-content-between align-items-start">
            <div class="ms-2 me-auto">
              <div class="fw-semibold">Unzip both packages into the temporary directory</div>
              <div class="small text-body-secondary mt-1">
                <code>C:\&gt; PKUNZIP TurboPascal-Unpack.zip C:\TPATCH</code><br>
                <code>C:\&gt; PKUNZIP crtfix16.zip C:\TPATCH</code>
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="list-group-item d-flex justify-content-between align-items-start">
            <div class="ms-2 me-auto">
              <div class="fw-semibold">Decompress the target executable</div>
              <div class="small text-body-secondary my-1">
                In this example we patch <code>defacto.exe</code>, an early Defacto magazine binary that suffers from the bug.
              </div>
              <code>C:\&gt; UNP DEFACTO.EXE</code>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="list-group-item d-flex justify-content-between align-items-start">
            <div class="ms-2 me-auto">
              <div class="fw-semibold">Apply the fix</div>
              <div class="my-1"><code>C:\&gt; CRTFIX DEFACTO.EXE</code></div>
              <div class="small text-body-secondary">
                If CRTFix returns <q>Fixing code... Fixed</q>, the binary is now patched and the runtime error 200 crashes should be gone.
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </article>

      <article id="invalid-page-fault" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Windows 95 / 98</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Invalid page fault in module</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            The invalid page fault alert is an error caused by programs compiled for Windows 95 and 98 attempting to access restricted memory.
            This is commonly an emulation or virtualization issue, and often the only solution is to run the program on real PC hardware.
            Alternatively, the binary may be corrupt due to antiquated antivirus removal.
          </p>
        </div>
        <figure class="figure d-block text-center mt-4 mb-0">
          <img src="/image/new/pagefault.png" loading="lazy" class="img-fluid rounded border shadow-sm" alt="Screenshot of a program running in Windows 98 causing an invalid page fault prompt">
          <figcaption class="figure-caption mt-2">Windows 98 invalid page fault dialog.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <div id="missing-dlls" class="row g-4" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;">
        <div class="col-md-6">
          <article class="changelog-entry p-4 h-100 d-flex flex-column">
            <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Windows DLL · Audio</div>
            <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">NPMOD32.DLL is missing</h2>
            <p class="small flex-grow-1">
              <code>NPMOD32.DLL</code> is a Windows library designed to play older <a href="https://www.modarchive.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">module music</a> files popular in the demo and music scenes.
              Sometimes coders forgot to bundle this file with their application. Copy the DLL into the same directory as the application to fix the error.
            </p>
            <div class="border-top pt-3 mt-2">
              <a href="/f/a53f43" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary">Download NPMOD32.DLL</a>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
          <article class="changelog-entry p-4 h-100 d-flex flex-column">
            <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Windows DLL · DirectX</div>
            <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">D3DRM.DLL not loaded</h2>
            <p class="small flex-grow-1">
              <code>D3DRM.DLL</code> is a Windows DirectX library included in early versions of the API and removed in Windows XP and later.
              Copy the DLL into the same directory as the application to resolve the missing library error.
            </p>
            <div class="border-top pt-3 mt-2">
              <a href="/f/a643bb" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary">Download D3DRM.DLL</a>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>

